## What happened

Apple's security frameworks (Security.framework / CommonCrypto) dropped DSA support. .NET on macOS delegates to these native libraries, so `DSA.Create()` now throws **`PlatformNotSupportedException`** on macOS. Linux (OpenSSL) and Windows (CNG) still support it, which is why only macOS breaks.

DSA has been marked obsolete since .NET 7 (`SYSLIB0028`), and .NET 11 removed the macOS fallback entirely.

## How to fix it

**Option A — Managed implementation (quick fix for legacy verification):**
Use **BouncyCastle**, which provides a pure managed DSA that works everywhere:

```bash
dotnet add package BouncyCastle.Cryptography
```

```csharp
using Org.BouncyCastle.Crypto.Parameters;
using Org.BouncyCastle.Crypto.Signers;
using Org.BouncyCastle.Security;

// Import your existing DSA public key
var publicKey = (DsaPublicKeyParameters)PublicKeyFactory.CreateKey(publicKeyBytes);
var signer = new DsaSigner();
signer.Init(forSigning: false, publicKey);

// Verify signature
bool valid = signer.VerifySignature(hash, r, s);
```

**Option B — Migrate away from DSA (recommended long-term):**
DSA is a legacy algorithm. Re-sign with **ECDSA** or **Ed25519**, both of which are fully supported cross-platform in .NET:

```csharp
using var ecdsa = ECDsa.Create(ECCurve.NamedCurves.nistP256);
```

**Recommendation:** If you control the signing side, migrate to ECDSA/EdDSA. If you must verify existing DSA signatures you can't re-sign, use BouncyCastle as a cross-platform managed fallback.
